From: Wayland Cursor Fix <local@localhost>
Date: Mon, 03 Aug 2026 11:45:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] xwayland: skip the release warp while re-grabbed

Xwayland delays showing a newly visible cursor for up to 5 ms. If the
application re-grabs and re-hides the pointer during that interval (the
Badlion/Minecraft menu spam pattern), the delayed show can still fire and
run the revision-8 release branch while the new capture is active. That
destroys the new capture's pointer lock and teleports the real pointer
to the stale release target. The camera phase then runs without any
pointer constraint and the following release restores nothing, which
reads as a sudden large pointer displacement and a lost reset.

Only run the release branch when no explicit pointer grab is active.
With a grab active the lock and emulator belong to the ongoing capture
and are kept alive, as in the revision-4 semantics.
---
 hw/xwayland/xwayland-input.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/xwayland/xwayland-input.c b/hw/xwayland/xwayland-input.c
--- a/hw/xwayland/xwayland-input.c
+++ b/hw/xwayland/xwayland-input.c
@@ -3835,6 +3835,16 @@
         int x, y;
 
         /*
+         * A delayed cursor show can fire while the application has
+         * already re-grabbed the pointer for continued capture. Running
+         * the release sequence now would destroy the new capture's
+         * lock and teleport the pointer to the stale release target.
+         * Nothing is released in that state; keep the emulator alive.
+         */
+        if (xwl_seat_has_explicit_pointer_grab(xwl_seat))
+            return;
+
+        /*
          * A direct warp after unlocking reliably restores the most recent
          * virtual X11 position, even if the application sends no final warp.
          *
